<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">How it works</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Places you name") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Your town, the one you drive to on weekends, where the grandparents
        live, the city you're visiting in March. A place is a point on a map
        the app already carries — nothing is looked up anywhere.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Calendars you follow") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Under each place, the calendars that publish what's on there: the
        library, the city, a Meetup group, a venue's upcoming page. Paste an
        address and the calendar behind it gets found. Only calendars
        publishers offer; nothing is scraped.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Judged, not filtered") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Your assistant reads each new listing against your hobbies and two
        sentences you write — what else you'd go to, and what you'd give
        time to — and says why. No keyword lists. Tell it “not this” and it
        remembers.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "A nudge, in time") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The ones that are for you become suggestions inside the lead time
        you set, on your phone if you've asked for that, and retire on their
        own the day the event passes. One tap puts it on your calendar.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Volunteering, and trips</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    A volunteer role has no date, so it gets its own shape: a “weekend dog
    walkers wanted” listing is judged against what you said you'd give
    time to, shown with your people, and lands as a task rather than a
    calendar entry. And a trip's stay counts as a place for its nights, so
    a city you'll be in next month shows what's on while you're there.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">It never searches for you</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Asking a service for “events near me” would send your location and your
    interests to somebody else's server, on a timer, forever. This reads the
    calendars you chose instead, from your own box. The one exception is a
    ticketing search you can turn on per place, with a plain sentence on
    the switch saying exactly what it sends — and it's off until you do.
  </p>
</section>
